EXT. RAILROAD CROSSING - FIELD TRIP BUS - DAY

A yellow school bus idles at a country crossing. Barn-red freight cars clatter by, one after another, endless. Heat shimmers over the tracks. The bus is empty but for two.

ROSCOE LANGSTON, 13, in a buttoned-up shirt and a tie his mother chose, sits alone in the back with a field-trip permission slip folded in his fist. Across the aisle PHOEBE ABERNATHY-LIU, 13, in a denim vest with a patch for every band she has not heard, sprawls with her boots on the seat.

The train rattles on. Neither speaks. He steals a look. She steals one back.

ROSCOE
Where's everyone?

PHOEBE
Museum.

ROSCOE
Why aren't we at the museum?

PHOEBE
Because I threw up.

ROSCOE
And me?

PHOEBE
Because you volunteered to stay with me.

ROSCOE
I did?

PHOEBE
You raised your hand.

He thinks about this. His ears go red.

ROSCOE
Oh.

She grins. Props her chin on her fist. He fiddles with his tie. The train keeps coming.

PHOEBE
Have you ever counted them?

ROSCOE
The cars?

PHOEBE
Yeah.

ROSCOE
No.

PHOEBE
Want to?

ROSCOE
Okay.

They press their faces to the glass. Their lips move. Two voices, soft, almost in unison.

ROSCOE (CONT'D)
Forty-one. Forty-two.

PHOEBE
Forty-three.

ROSCOE
Forty-four.

She leans her shoulder against his. He freezes. Does not pull away. The train goes on and on.

PHOEBE
Sixty.

ROSCOE
Sixty-one.

PHOEBE
Roscoe.

ROSCOE
Yeah?

PHOEBE
Don't lose count.

He doesn't. The train thunders past, the whole bus trembles, and a line of sunlit boxcars flickers over their faces like a film.

ROSCOE
Eighty-eight.

PHOEBE
Eighty-nine.

Her hand finds his on the seat. He turns his over. She laces her fingers with his. Neither looks away from the window.

ROSCOE
Ninety.

PHOEBE
Ninety-one.

The last car passes. Silence rolls in behind it, long and bright. A crossing bell stops. Gates rise.

ROSCOE
Ninety-three.

PHOEBE
Ninety-three.

She does not let go. The bus engine coughs. The driver, offscreen, honks twice. They stay where they are a moment longer.
